								<div class="title" id="post-title-4835426">
																	<a href="/scp-4485/comments/show#post-4835426"></a>
									</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/tl333s" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1304786); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1304786&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607024358" alt="TL333s" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1304786)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/tl333s" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1304786); return false;" >TL333s</a></span> <span class="odate time_1607024192 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">03 Dec 2020 19:36</span>
																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-50742/per-page-discussions">Per Page Discussions / Per page discussions</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/scp-4485/comments/show">SCP-4485</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4835426">
									

<p>Quite the effort! I wanted to comment first on the formatting, keeping in mind I'm using Chrome on a Windows PC. The article of the body itself is just fine, the early parts especially feeling like reading microfiche. And I love the &quot;submit approval&quot; doodad.</p>
<p>However, the right-side footnote pop-ups tended to leave graphical garbage behind when popping back in. And the hidden left-side panel, which a cool idea, moves in a herky-jerky way that rather undermines the aesthetic if you go scoping it out. Not sure if there's anything that can be done about that.</p>
<p>As for the story itself, I love the marriage of CotBG and AWCY. The story of how HANSARP comes together is the meat of this, and I really enjoyed the background. &quot;Cosmic Top-Secret&quot; is, I'm not gonna lie, pretty hokey. But from the outset, I got the sense that something was wrong, maybe the Foundation had been infiltrated or infected by something, and I though I never quite figured it out, all the talk of 'taint' and the dissolution of the Ethics Committee was massively thrilling. Also, the survey is terrifying, gj.</p>
<p>Thank goodness part 3 was the last real bit, though. I was beginning to agree with Dr. Lark that it was getting long.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">A&nbsp;couple&nbsp;stnanks</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>In offset 2, Switzerland is listed as both a primary and additional area of activity?</p>
<p>I didn't note the offset, but at one point, the ethics council (why council?) is said to have been &quot;desolved&quot;. Intentional?</p>
<p>From offset 5: &quot;who now a functional super-intelligence&quot;</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>But if nothing else, credit must be given to the authors for writing a historical skip that takes place mostly in Germany and never once bringing up Nazis. :') +1</p>

							</div>

								
					
